1
00:00:04,833 --> 00:00:06,508
Molco.

2
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:10,956
Sorry.

3
00:00:15,036 --> 00:00:18,156
Well, the meeting at 11 o'clock....

4
00:00:20,076 --> 00:00:22,566
Did you just take a bath with Molco?

5
00:00:22,566 --> 00:00:25,068
Because Molco can't take a bath on her own.

6
00:00:30,464 --> 00:00:32,556
All right then, let's begin.

7
00:00:32,556 --> 00:00:34,050
We're starting now.

8
00:00:35,256 --> 00:00:37,033
So, to formally kick things off,

9
00:00:37,033 --> 00:00:40,963
here is the concept for the new character
that Seo Design will create.

10
00:00:42,408 --> 00:00:44,656
It's "Just Support."

11
00:00:44,656 --> 00:00:46,760
Not pushing you forward, not being cynical,

12
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:49,320
just simply supporting you and staying close.

13
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:52,972
So, Mr. Seo, we'll have you start
drawing the design right away.

14
00:00:53,864 --> 00:00:58,368
At the same time,
you'll also do work to earn a living.

15
00:00:58,368 --> 00:01:01,576
And in fact, we've received a new request

16
00:01:01,576 --> 00:01:04,864
from Oyama Bakery
to create a character for their shop.

17
00:01:04,864 --> 00:01:08,468
So grateful... Are you listening?

18
00:01:09,344 --> 00:01:13,288
Seriously, taking Molco
without asking is already a crime.

19
00:01:13,288 --> 00:01:16,288
On top of that, you're slow to act.
That's a serious offense.

20
00:01:16,288 --> 00:01:17,888
Slow to act?

21
00:01:17,888 --> 00:01:21,116
Because if you had helped me faster
with a swift move back then...

22
00:01:23,104 --> 00:01:25,848
There's no way I could have been
faster than that. It was so sudden.

23
00:01:25,848 --> 00:01:27,378
I can do it, you know.

24
00:01:27,378 --> 00:01:30,080
Swish! Did you see that just now?

25
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:32,370
I think I'm faster.

26
00:01:32,370 --> 00:01:33,867
Swish.

27
00:01:33,867 --> 00:01:34,930
Wasn't that so fast you couldn't even see it?

28
00:01:34,930 --> 00:01:36,640
So slow! Too slow to see.

29
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:38,100
Alright, you try it then.

30
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:39,420
Swish... See?

31
00:01:39,420 --> 00:01:42,167
- That's five times! Ten times...
- No, no. Wrong, that's wrong.

32
00:01:42,870 --> 00:01:45,800
Cute.

33
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:48,290
- Please have a seat here.
- Thank you.

34
00:01:49,690 --> 00:01:51,030
Delicious.

35
00:01:51,030 --> 00:01:53,350
You're still at it, right?
The study of cuteness.

36
00:01:53,350 --> 00:01:55,340
Oh, Mr. Hirose.

37
00:01:55,960 --> 00:01:57,460
All right then,
I'll have the steamed chicken salad

38
00:01:57,460 --> 00:01:59,470
that's gentle on the stomach.

39
00:01:59,470 --> 00:02:01,470
Certainly.

40
00:02:03,630 --> 00:02:06,230
If I recall, what you wanted to know

41
00:02:06,230 --> 00:02:09,130
- is that cuteness beyond logic, right?
- Yes.

42
00:02:09,130 --> 00:02:12,167
Like being obsessed with it
while waking or sleeping,

43
00:02:12,167 --> 00:02:14,967
so much so that you could even call it love.

44
00:02:14,967 --> 00:02:17,080
Well, someone once said it.

45
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:20,233
Love has no logic, comes out of nowhere,

46
00:02:20,233 --> 00:02:23,180
and it's like getting struck by lightning.

47
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:24,767
Lightning...

48
00:02:24,767 --> 00:02:30,800
By the way, for me, the lightning
is this character's right here!

49
00:02:31,433 --> 00:02:36,943
The moment I saw it, it was like,
"Flash! Rumble! Bang! I love it!"

50
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:41,133
Incidentally, the trendiest young people

51
00:02:41,133 --> 00:02:44,512
apparently call falling out of love
the "frog-ification phenomenon."

52
00:02:44,512 --> 00:02:46,112
Ribbit, ribbit.

53
00:02:46,112 --> 00:02:48,967
- Ribbit, ribbit...
- Oh, come on, that's outdated.

54
00:02:48,967 --> 00:02:50,833
What?

55
00:02:51,833 --> 00:02:56,200
Also, there's something else
I'd like to ask you about.

56
00:02:56,200 --> 00:02:57,952
Please, ask away.

57
00:02:57,952 --> 00:03:02,267
Well, actually, we're working on
creating a new character,

58
00:03:02,267 --> 00:03:04,600
and I'm wondering how to go about its debut.

59
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:08,167
But when it comes to money
and things like that... You know.

60
00:03:08,167 --> 00:03:14,133
In that case, maybe low-cost options
like stamps or selling illustrations online.

61
00:03:14,133 --> 00:03:19,616
If you can spend a bit more,
I'd recommend acrylic keychains or stickers.

62
00:03:19,616 --> 00:03:23,233
Right. That's definitely
the classic route, isn't it?

63
00:03:23,233 --> 00:03:26,000
Sorry, I can only give you standard advice.

64
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,500
Oh, I'm sorry! An amateur like me
asking a planner from Merry...

65
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:30,967
- Oh, no.
- I'm sorry.

66
00:03:30,967 --> 00:03:33,637
While I am a planner, at Merry,

67
00:03:33,637 --> 00:03:36,300
the president handles
important projects directly.

68
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:38,900
Besides, I'm not really good at
that kind of thing to begin with.

69
00:03:38,900 --> 00:03:41,600
You know, when I first joined the company,
I was a pattern maker.

70
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:43,872
Oh, do you know what that is? Well...

71
00:03:43,872 --> 00:03:47,067
It's like making
the paper patterns for plushies.

72
00:03:49,267 --> 00:03:52,667
- Plushies.
- Plushies.

73
00:03:53,367 --> 00:03:56,533
- Plushies.
- Plushies.

74
00:03:57,433 --> 00:03:59,300
Plushies!

75
00:03:59,300 --> 00:04:03,033
[Touch it, and you're fired]
Mr. Seo, let's debut with a plushie.

76
00:04:03,700 --> 00:04:05,467
[Plushie Debut]
A plushie?

77
00:04:05,467 --> 00:04:09,533
It's true that standard debuts
are low-cost and online,

78
00:04:09,533 --> 00:04:11,503
but a small player should play its part.

79
00:04:11,503 --> 00:04:14,300
We should have a debut that
has a hook and isn't the standard route.

80
00:04:14,300 --> 00:04:18,033
So, that's why we're going to
be bold and debut with a plushie!

81
00:04:18,033 --> 00:04:19,833
Alright, alright, alright.

82
00:04:19,833 --> 00:04:22,688
And besides, Mr. Seo,
you love plushies, don't you? See?

83
00:04:22,688 --> 00:04:24,000
Don't you have an unusual fondness for them?

84
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:25,433
Alright, alright, alright.

85
00:04:25,433 --> 00:04:28,733
[Just Support]
And above all, don't you think a plushie

86
00:04:28,733 --> 00:04:30,867
is a perfect match with this?

87
00:04:30,867 --> 00:04:33,533
When you want to support someone,
when you want to be by their side,

88
00:04:33,533 --> 00:04:37,333
isn't being able to
physically touch them important?

89
00:04:37,333 --> 00:04:40,800
Isn't being able to stay with them
for a long time important?

90
00:04:41,467 --> 00:04:46,400
Well, there is a certain bliss
in not knowing anything,

91
00:04:46,400 --> 00:04:48,300
but when it comes to
actually making a plushie,

92
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:49,600
- it's not just the designer.
- Mr. Seo.

93
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:51,744
- We need that person...
- Mr. Seo.

94
00:04:51,744 --> 00:04:54,644
- Well, and that person...
- Mr. Seo.

95
00:04:55,267 --> 00:04:58,348
That makes you grin with anticipation.

96
00:04:59,067 --> 00:05:00,833
What?

97
00:05:00,833 --> 00:05:02,567
Why would I grin with anticipation?

98
00:05:02,567 --> 00:05:04,940
Let's do it. The plushie debut.

99
00:05:07,100 --> 00:05:08,767
No, no, that's impossible.

100
00:05:08,767 --> 00:05:10,867
Plushie debut.

101
00:05:13,467 --> 00:05:16,300
Yeah! We're doing a plushie debut!

102
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:29,216
Once the concept is set, we decide
the size, price range, and usage.

103
00:05:29,216 --> 00:05:31,300
[Episode 3]
Then create the character design.

104
00:05:31,300 --> 00:05:34,367
We specify the three-view drawings and colors
for the front, side, and back,

105
00:05:34,367 --> 00:05:36,733
consider the image of the materials,
and design the three-dimensional model.

106
00:05:36,733 --> 00:05:39,333
- After all that back and forth, even...
- But if you look online,

107
00:05:39,333 --> 00:05:43,700
you see sites that seem able to
make plushies much more easily.

108
00:05:43,700 --> 00:05:46,900
That's fine for personal enjoyment,

109
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:50,900
but toy stores and such must be able to
guarantee that they were made properly,

110
00:05:50,900 --> 00:05:53,667
due to safety concerns and the like.

111
00:05:53,667 --> 00:05:58,233
Which means that Cynical Molco
can be placed in stores,

112
00:05:58,233 --> 00:06:02,380
but this Mugi can't.

113
00:06:02,380 --> 00:06:06,113
- What? Is that... Me?
- Yeah.

114
00:06:07,633 --> 00:06:10,533
- Victory.
- Love.

115
00:06:11,400 --> 00:06:12,733
- Cute.
- But

116
00:06:12,733 --> 00:06:16,033
proper manufacturers usually
only take large orders,

117
00:06:16,033 --> 00:06:19,567
and that will affect the price.

118
00:06:19,567 --> 00:06:24,433
We have to do our other jobs properly
and make sure to earn enough money.

119
00:06:25,133 --> 00:06:27,100
Good!

120
00:06:27,100 --> 00:06:29,460
This is really good!

121
00:06:29,460 --> 00:06:32,133
Does "good" mean "cute"?

122
00:06:32,133 --> 00:06:36,300
Of course it's cute.
Like... in a perfectly normal way.

123
00:06:36,300 --> 00:06:39,733
Alright, I'll show this to Mr. Oyama then.

124
00:06:39,733 --> 00:06:44,096
By the way... what about
the new character design?

125
00:06:44,096 --> 00:06:46,933
Obviously, It's not done yet. Obviously.

126
00:06:46,933 --> 00:06:49,233
I see. How long will it be
until it's finished?

127
00:06:49,233 --> 00:06:52,700
Someone who can answer that question
is a half-baked amateur, not a pro.

128
00:06:52,700 --> 00:06:55,933
But if we don't even know that,
it'll be hard for us to move forward.

129
00:06:55,933 --> 00:06:57,833
Wait... Where are you going?

130
00:06:57,833 --> 00:07:01,820
Someone who can answer that question
is a half-baked amateur, not a pro.

131
00:07:01,820 --> 00:07:03,160
Well, why don't we go for lunch?

132
00:07:03,160 --> 00:07:06,100
- We can finalize the schedule then...
- My grandma used to say that

133
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:11,500
every meal you eat with someone
from work turns into poop.

134
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:13,520
Well...

135
00:07:15,280 --> 00:07:20,133
One week! The deadline
is in one week! I've said it!

136
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:25,067
[Original Character]
Nice! Really nice.

137
00:07:25,067 --> 00:07:27,167
And the fact that you finished it
before the deadline is nice too.

138
00:07:27,167 --> 00:07:31,267
- I am truly sorry about last time...
- No, no, I'm just kidding, just kidding.

139
00:07:31,267 --> 00:07:34,080
So, may I ask for a few minor corrections?

140
00:07:34,080 --> 00:07:36,000
Please, tell me.

141
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:38,967
Well, first, about this beard...

142
00:07:38,967 --> 00:07:42,000
Yeah, I think it's better than before.

143
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:46,600
What? I think my back
is feeling better than before.

144
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:53,433
[Harsh Review Molco]
Well, what is this?

145
00:07:53,433 --> 00:07:56,267
You mean Harsh Review Molco?

146
00:07:56,267 --> 00:07:59,800
I thought I'd add a hidden backstory
to Cynical Molco.

147
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:01,267
A hidden backstory...

148
00:08:01,267 --> 00:08:06,400
It was urgent, so Ms. Sasaoka and I
just put it together for now.

149
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:11,780
As you may know,
Molco-related sales are starting to stagnate.

150
00:08:11,780 --> 00:08:14,100
Well, that may be, but it's still
maintaining high figures.

151
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:17,132
But you know what will happen
if people get tired of it, right?

152
00:08:17,888 --> 00:08:19,468
But...

153
00:08:20,967 --> 00:08:25,452
I'd like you to help from now on, Mr. Hirose.

154
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,400
I'd be happy to.

155
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:31,867
At the same time as we announce
Harsh Review Molco,

156
00:08:31,867 --> 00:08:35,000
we'll also distribute plushies to
all stores at once and boost our numbers.

157
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:36,300
I'm expecting great things.

158
00:08:36,300 --> 00:08:39,800
Leave it to me! A rising dragon...

159
00:08:42,720 --> 00:08:48,060
[Day 1]

160
00:08:48,060 --> 00:08:49,733
[Day 2]
That's so like you.

161
00:08:49,733 --> 00:08:51,168
[Day 3]
I'm craving curry.

162
00:08:51,168 --> 00:08:52,224
[Day 4]

163
00:08:52,224 --> 00:08:54,624
[Day 5]
Would you like to take a photo? Meow!

164
00:08:54,624 --> 00:08:55,733
Meow.

165
00:08:55,733 --> 00:08:57,400
[Day 6]
What do you want to do?

166
00:08:57,400 --> 00:09:00,352
[Day 7]

167
00:09:00,352 --> 00:09:03,136
Welcome.

168
00:09:03,136 --> 00:09:06,033
Welcome.

169
00:09:11,552 --> 00:09:13,667
No!

170
00:09:13,667 --> 00:09:17,472
So, it's been a week now.
Show me the new character design.

171
00:09:17,472 --> 00:09:20,268
Drink Zapaan, Zapaan...

172
00:09:21,700 --> 00:09:24,760
What? Was that deadline a week?

173
00:09:24,760 --> 00:09:26,890
Ah, I thought you were saying

174
00:09:26,890 --> 00:09:29,740
- "Deadline Ikkyu" or something.
- The design.

175
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:40,933
Is this... baby sardines?

176
00:09:40,933 --> 00:09:44,480
That's why I said there's no way
I can draw it that easily.

177
00:09:44,480 --> 00:09:46,067
Were you even working on it
properly to begin with?

178
00:09:46,067 --> 00:09:48,967
To me, you just looked like
a wandering man who loves taking walks.

179
00:09:48,967 --> 00:09:51,600
I mean, this is our own project,

180
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:53,567
and it's not like we're
causing trouble for anyone else.

181
00:09:53,567 --> 00:09:56,192
- So all this nagging about deadlines...
- Alright,

182
00:09:56,192 --> 00:09:58,633
do it in three days.

183
00:09:58,633 --> 00:10:01,696
That's why I said there's no way
I can draw it that easily...

184
00:10:01,696 --> 00:10:05,164
Anyway, just draw something. In three days.

185
00:10:05,867 --> 00:10:08,900
Yeah, it's baby sardines.

186
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:15,100
Look, without the new character design,

187
00:10:15,100 --> 00:10:18,912
we can't negotiate with
merchandise manufacturers...

188
00:10:18,912 --> 00:10:22,232
But, you know, it's precisely
by transcending such common sense

189
00:10:22,232 --> 00:10:23,500
that one becomes a pro.

190
00:10:23,500 --> 00:10:29,933
That's what I think makes a pro.
Right? Huh? Am I wrong?

191
00:10:29,933 --> 00:10:32,064
In other words, saying that kind of thing

192
00:10:32,064 --> 00:10:33,767
means you're not a professional consultant.

193
00:10:33,767 --> 00:10:36,567
- You're more like a professional dud...
- I understand.

194
00:10:36,567 --> 00:10:40,233
If negotiations with the manufacturer
go well and we secure a factory,

195
00:10:40,233 --> 00:10:42,233
deadlines must be strictly observed.

196
00:10:42,233 --> 00:10:44,233
They must be observed with your life.

197
00:10:44,233 --> 00:10:49,867
But... well, it'll be fine, right, Mr. Seo?
You're a pro, after all!

198
00:10:49,867 --> 00:10:52,800
What? You mean you
don't even have a design yet?

199
00:10:52,800 --> 00:10:57,500
Sorry to bother you. I just had to
speak with you, so I reached out.

200
00:10:57,500 --> 00:11:01,500
No... An order of just 100 units.

201
00:11:03,100 --> 00:11:05,200
Just out of curiosity,

202
00:11:05,200 --> 00:11:08,867
if you were to accept the production,
what's the fastest you could start?

203
00:11:08,867 --> 00:11:12,920
Well... at the earliest,
maybe a year from now.

204
00:11:12,920 --> 00:11:14,800
What?

205
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:17,833
[Hasegawa Textile Studio Co., Ltd.]
Two years from now.

206
00:11:17,833 --> 00:11:21,767
- Two...
- Sorry we couldn't be of help.

207
00:11:26,500 --> 00:11:30,400
Well, it's true that our plushie order
is a small quantity.

208
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:33,867
That's precisely why it fits perfectly
into gaps of hours or days

209
00:11:33,867 --> 00:11:37,300
that can't be filled by large orders.

210
00:11:37,300 --> 00:11:42,300
By not letting any machines sit idle at all,
we can maximize your company's profits.

211
00:11:42,300 --> 00:11:45,067
You just don't get it.

212
00:11:45,067 --> 00:11:49,100
Really. You don't get it at all.

213
00:11:49,100 --> 00:11:51,633
Really not get it at all?

214
00:11:53,080 --> 00:11:54,080
What?

215
00:11:54,080 --> 00:11:56,433
Don't you remember me?

216
00:11:57,100 --> 00:11:59,100
I've never seen your face before.

217
00:11:59,100 --> 00:12:04,067
Oh... I see. Well, no matter.

218
00:12:04,067 --> 00:12:08,064
But these little guys sure are cute.

219
00:12:08,064 --> 00:12:11,168
This one here looks like
that thing, doesn't it?

220
00:12:11,168 --> 00:12:14,900
You know, that thing...
Well... Cynical Molco.

221
00:12:14,900 --> 00:12:18,533
But I think this one is cuter.

222
00:12:18,533 --> 00:12:24,433
Even though it's a subtle difference,
the aura it gives off is much softer.

223
00:12:24,433 --> 00:12:25,540
You do get it.

224
00:12:25,540 --> 00:12:27,100
What? I do?

225
00:12:27,100 --> 00:12:29,920
Do I get it more than the person
you said doesn't get it?

226
00:12:29,920 --> 00:12:31,433
You get it. You get it.

227
00:12:31,433 --> 00:12:35,633
That person doesn't get it at all
and is slow to act.

228
00:12:35,633 --> 00:12:37,397
What? That person doesn't get it

229
00:12:37,397 --> 00:12:39,167
- and is slow to act?
- Yes.

230
00:12:39,167 --> 00:12:43,900
If I'm... for example... Swish! Swish!

231
00:12:43,900 --> 00:12:45,868
That person is just...

232
00:12:47,264 --> 00:12:50,720
- "Pa." Just "Pa."
- "Pa"?

233
00:12:50,720 --> 00:12:52,204
Like that.

234
00:12:57,267 --> 00:12:59,520
- Ms. Kusakabe.
- Yes?

235
00:12:59,520 --> 00:13:03,468
Well, this might actually be
an unreasonable request from me...

236
00:13:06,567 --> 00:13:08,608
Are you really sure about this?

237
00:13:08,608 --> 00:13:12,867
- If you fail, it'll be a sopping mess...
- It's fine. I'll save it before then.

238
00:13:12,867 --> 00:13:15,000
Like a cat. Swish!

239
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,920
Alright, alright.

240
00:13:17,920 --> 00:13:21,452
Alright. Here I go.

241
00:13:36,367 --> 00:13:39,000
Swish!

242
00:13:55,700 --> 00:13:57,267
Are you okay?

243
00:13:57,267 --> 00:13:59,852
- Swish! Swish...
- What? Wait...

244
00:14:26,464 --> 00:14:27,600
Hello?

245
00:14:27,600 --> 00:14:30,040
Good work. Mr. Seo, I secured it.

246
00:14:30,040 --> 00:14:32,400
<i>A plushie factory.</i>

247
00:14:32,400 --> 00:14:35,740
<i>It seems there was a sudden cancellation.</i>

248
00:14:35,740 --> 00:14:37,006
Well... I see.

249
00:14:37,006 --> 00:14:39,567
But the situation has gotten a little tight.

250
00:14:39,567 --> 00:14:43,233
It seems we only have 4 days left
to finalize the design.

251
00:14:43,233 --> 00:14:45,536
[Anna Kusakabe]
<i>But, well, it'll be fine, right, Mr. Seo?</i>

252
00:14:45,536 --> 00:14:47,936
<i>You're a pro, after all.</i>

253
00:14:47,936 --> 00:14:50,300
<i>What's wrong?</i>

254
00:14:50,300 --> 00:14:53,033
- <i>Are you okay?</i>
- Well...

255
00:14:53,033 --> 00:14:54,784
<i>Are you okay?</i>

256
00:14:54,784 --> 00:14:58,100
- Thank...
- Mr. Seo.

257
00:15:06,767 --> 00:15:11,533
I have no words for such a stupid reason.

258
00:15:12,533 --> 00:15:14,533
I said it's fine.

259
00:15:14,533 --> 00:15:16,300
Look, look.

260
00:15:16,300 --> 00:15:18,900
Look, look, look.

261
00:15:21,800 --> 00:15:23,468
Swish!

262
00:15:24,733 --> 00:15:29,133
Anyway, just focus on your treatment for now.

263
00:15:29,133 --> 00:15:31,833
Once you're able to draw again,

264
00:15:31,833 --> 00:15:33,728
figure out how many days
are left until the deadline,

265
00:15:33,728 --> 00:15:35,933
and judge if you can still make it.

266
00:15:35,933 --> 00:15:38,800
If it's no good, we'll have to
apologize to Mr. Oyama,

267
00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:42,967
and notify the plushie factory.

268
00:15:43,733 --> 00:15:46,080
Even though we finally secured it.

269
00:15:46,080 --> 00:15:51,433
Well, there are other places
that will make them for us.

270
00:15:51,433 --> 00:15:54,700
After all, how many of those companies
are there nationwide?

271
00:15:54,700 --> 00:15:57,400
Those manufacturers? Factories?

272
00:15:57,400 --> 00:15:59,700
We haven't contacted all of them, right?

273
00:15:59,700 --> 00:16:01,600
There is, there is.
There's got to be one somewhere.

274
00:16:01,600 --> 00:16:05,533
- So cheer up.
- Then why don't you try finding one?

275
00:16:07,167 --> 00:16:08,880
Well? What?

276
00:16:08,880 --> 00:16:11,900
If it's so easy to say,
why don't you find one yourself?

277
00:16:11,900 --> 00:16:14,560
No, no, what are you talking about?
That's your job, isn't it?

278
00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:16,367
Right. There's no way you could do it, right?

279
00:16:16,367 --> 00:16:17,756
It's impossible for you,

280
00:16:17,756 --> 00:16:19,136
whose communication skills
are as tiny as baby sardines...

281
00:16:19,136 --> 00:16:20,864
I can do it. I mean, I'm always doing it!

282
00:16:20,864 --> 00:16:24,320
Oh, can you get three companies
on board in three days?

283
00:16:24,320 --> 00:16:26,816
No, ten. For me,
it's ten companies in three days.

284
00:16:26,816 --> 00:16:29,467
Wow, what a pro. I have to
learn a thing or two from you.

285
00:16:29,467 --> 00:16:32,433
Well then, you have three days
for the Oyama Bakery revisions.

286
00:16:32,433 --> 00:16:35,267
What? What? What kind of logic is that?

287
00:16:35,267 --> 00:16:36,933
What? You can't draw the design?

288
00:16:36,933 --> 00:16:38,336
Because you are always "Draw, draw!"

289
00:16:38,336 --> 00:16:40,000
with an "I could totally draw too"
look on your face.

290
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:41,467
So I naturally assumed you could draw it.

291
00:16:41,467 --> 00:16:42,656
Oh, now I get it!

292
00:16:42,656 --> 00:16:45,376
You're just a mouth-only consultant.
A mouth-sultant.

293
00:16:45,376 --> 00:16:47,733
The "ku" in Kusakabe is for mouth.
Mouth-sultant.

294
00:16:47,733 --> 00:16:49,536
You're a mouth-sultant, Kusakabe.

295
00:16:49,536 --> 00:16:52,733
I can do it. I can mostly do it,
thanks for asking.

296
00:16:52,733 --> 00:16:55,400
A pro? Well, then, starting tomorrow...

297
00:16:55,400 --> 00:16:58,380
- I'll be counting on you.
- I'll be counting on you.

298
00:17:42,467 --> 00:17:47,533
Well, so the design isn't ready yet,
and there's no money.

299
00:17:47,533 --> 00:17:51,767
And both your hands... what happened?

300
00:17:51,767 --> 00:17:54,300
But I'll definitely draw something good.

301
00:17:54,300 --> 00:17:56,608
And as the manufacturer
that makes our plushies,

302
00:17:56,608 --> 00:17:59,360
your sales will also go boom!

303
00:17:59,360 --> 00:18:01,408
And you're talking casually?

304
00:18:01,408 --> 00:18:05,400
Ah, sorry, sorry. Sorry... May I...

305
00:18:05,400 --> 00:18:07,084
"May I..."

306
00:18:24,867 --> 00:18:26,188
It's done.

307
00:18:27,767 --> 00:18:29,100
What?

308
00:18:42,200 --> 00:18:44,420
<i>I wonder about this.</i>

309
00:18:44,420 --> 00:18:46,656
I feel like it's a bit different
from the design.

310
00:18:46,656 --> 00:18:50,720
Yeah. It looks a little too harsh.

311
00:18:50,720 --> 00:18:54,700
Well, I think it might be the eyes...

312
00:18:54,700 --> 00:18:56,236
The eyes?

313
00:19:35,933 --> 00:19:38,572
- A mole?
- It's me, Kusakabe.

314
00:19:40,800 --> 00:19:42,733
What is this?

315
00:19:42,733 --> 00:19:44,640
The original Mr. Oyama is completely gone.

316
00:19:44,640 --> 00:19:46,272
No, this is still, well, like a rough sketch.

317
00:19:46,272 --> 00:19:48,064
More like a croquis? Groggy?
Something like that.

318
00:19:48,064 --> 00:19:49,740
This is impossible.

319
00:20:00,233 --> 00:20:02,508
[Toylight Co., Ltd.]

320
00:20:10,933 --> 00:20:13,024
Haven't we refused you several times already?

321
00:20:13,024 --> 00:20:14,560
We told you it's impossible
to make every plushie

322
00:20:14,560 --> 00:20:16,640
with not even a 1mm margin of error.

323
00:20:16,640 --> 00:20:20,633
But look here!
The position of the eye is off by 1mm.

324
00:20:20,633 --> 00:20:22,933
That changes the impression it gives.

325
00:20:22,933 --> 00:20:25,824
So, with the quality of the factories
we contract with...

326
00:20:25,824 --> 00:20:29,200
But we want to deliver something
we're satisfied with,

327
00:20:29,200 --> 00:20:32,800
with our thoughts and sincerity,
to everyone who gets one of these.

328
00:20:32,800 --> 00:20:35,180
Don't you agree with that goal?

329
00:20:35,180 --> 00:20:37,680
Well, but even if you say that...

330
00:20:37,680 --> 00:20:41,100
Please, lend us your help!

331
00:20:41,100 --> 00:20:44,233
I'll try talking to the factory manager.

332
00:20:44,233 --> 00:20:45,700
Thank you very much.

333
00:20:45,700 --> 00:20:48,100
- Really, thank you.
- Well, no. It's still uncertain.

334
00:20:48,100 --> 00:20:51,367
No, I'm just grateful that
you're even willing to talk to them.

335
00:20:51,367 --> 00:20:53,548
- Well, please...
- That helps us...

336
00:20:55,733 --> 00:20:57,676
Mr. Seo?

337
00:20:59,640 --> 00:21:03,600
Wait... What happened to you?

338
00:21:03,600 --> 00:21:07,000
I see you're desperate as always.

339
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:08,400
What?

340
00:21:08,400 --> 00:21:11,616
Did you see that just now?
Oh, no, how embarrassing.

341
00:21:11,616 --> 00:21:14,200
It's nothing to be embarrassed about.

342
00:21:14,200 --> 00:21:17,484
You've always been desperate
about raising the numbers.

343
00:21:19,200 --> 00:21:22,667
Right. Well, it is work, after all.

344
00:21:22,667 --> 00:21:25,133
But ever since long ago,
you've also had an equally strong desire

345
00:21:25,133 --> 00:21:28,033
to deliver something good.

346
00:21:28,033 --> 00:21:35,167
So, do what you can
and fulfil your role properly.

347
00:21:36,467 --> 00:21:38,933
Surely everyone feels the same way,

348
00:21:38,933 --> 00:21:42,967
including the folks at Merry,
and of course, Ms. Kusakabe too.

349
00:21:45,900 --> 00:21:49,900
Mr. Seo, if there's anything I can do,
please tell me.

350
00:21:49,900 --> 00:21:52,204
I want to work with you again...

351
00:22:02,433 --> 00:22:03,756
Alright, then.

352
00:22:16,567 --> 00:22:17,900
Welcome back.

353
00:22:19,360 --> 00:22:22,720
- Excuse me for intruding.
- Oh, Mr. Hirose.

354
00:22:22,720 --> 00:22:24,467
What? Why?

355
00:22:24,467 --> 00:22:28,652
It's even more now. Even more polished.

356
00:22:32,544 --> 00:22:36,233
I'm sorry. I couldn't draw it.

357
00:22:39,968 --> 00:22:44,032
I'll contact Mr. Oyama later to reschedule.

358
00:22:44,032 --> 00:22:48,467
Why? The deadline is tomorrow, right?
We can do it.

359
00:22:48,467 --> 00:22:52,128
We... We... But Mr. Seo,
your hands are still like that.

360
00:22:52,128 --> 00:22:53,580
Excuse me.

361
00:22:55,633 --> 00:22:59,200
Heave-ho, here we go! Alright.

362
00:22:59,200 --> 00:23:00,716
Goodbye.

363
00:23:01,533 --> 00:23:05,184
First, I want to make the beard
look cleaner, so erase the edges too.

364
00:23:05,184 --> 00:23:06,304
Okay, okay.

365
00:23:06,304 --> 00:23:10,944
And as for the right eyebrow,
tilt it more to the right.

366
00:23:10,944 --> 00:23:13,452
- Okay, okay.
- Move it back 1mm.

367
00:23:14,240 --> 00:23:15,488
Okay, okay.

368
00:23:15,488 --> 00:23:17,344
Do the same for the other side.

369
00:23:17,344 --> 00:23:18,400
Okay.

370
00:23:18,400 --> 00:23:20,267
Mr. Oyama has double eyelids!

371
00:23:20,267 --> 00:23:21,600
Okay, okay.

372
00:23:21,600 --> 00:23:24,608
And put a little more of
a burn mark on the bread here.

373
00:23:24,608 --> 00:23:25,996
Okay, okay.

374
00:23:28,600 --> 00:23:29,600
The lower part too.

375
00:23:29,600 --> 00:23:32,384
Well... Here's a late-night snack.

376
00:23:32,384 --> 00:23:33,533
- Great.
- Really?

377
00:23:33,533 --> 00:23:35,900
- Hey, pass me that one.
- What?

378
00:23:35,900 --> 00:23:37,700
- How's this?
- Looks good.

379
00:23:37,700 --> 00:23:39,667
- The bread bun?
- No, that one.

380
00:23:39,667 --> 00:23:42,233
- That one.
- What? Which one?

381
00:23:42,233 --> 00:23:43,744
Nerimaru? Rinrin?

382
00:23:43,744 --> 00:23:45,667
Sorry, is it this one?

383
00:23:45,667 --> 00:23:46,880
Yes.

384
00:23:46,880 --> 00:23:50,368
I found this while looking for ideas.
You know that feeling of waves?

385
00:23:50,368 --> 00:23:52,544
I want it to hold milk that's full of energy.

386
00:23:52,544 --> 00:23:53,568
Oh, like this.

387
00:23:53,568 --> 00:23:56,332
So he wasn't just taking a walk.

388
00:23:58,720 --> 00:24:01,612
Yeah, I think it's good.

389
00:24:03,616 --> 00:24:05,420
That's amazing.

390
00:24:06,272 --> 00:24:10,800
You know, character designers
are really something.

391
00:24:23,800 --> 00:24:25,767
What should I do?

392
00:24:26,944 --> 00:24:28,032
Your hands!

393
00:24:28,032 --> 00:24:29,033
Ta-da!

394
00:24:29,033 --> 00:24:30,860
Are... Are you okay?

395
00:24:42,880 --> 00:24:45,068
- I'm sorry. Well then, I'm off.
- Alright.

396
00:24:45,867 --> 00:24:48,833
Hello, this is Hirose.

397
00:24:48,833 --> 00:24:51,367
Can you do it? Can you do the 1mm?

398
00:24:51,367 --> 00:24:54,600
Oh, thank you very much! Yes.

399
00:24:54,600 --> 00:24:56,780
Alright.

400
00:24:57,888 --> 00:25:02,367
It seems they'll be able to make
all the plushies without the 1mm error.

401
00:25:02,367 --> 00:25:06,668
But it seems that will cost a bit more,
so it'll need your approval.

402
00:25:08,367 --> 00:25:09,867
But this is great.

403
00:25:09,867 --> 00:25:13,600
With this, we can properly deliver
the feelings for the character

404
00:25:13,600 --> 00:25:17,292
- that you and Ms. Sasaoka put into it...
- If it's more expensive, let's not.

405
00:25:19,100 --> 00:25:22,240
Please proceed with
the originally estimated amount.

406
00:25:22,240 --> 00:25:23,776
Well, but...

407
00:25:23,776 --> 00:25:27,533
Nobody will notice a 1mm difference.

408
00:25:32,267 --> 00:25:33,867
- In terms of color...
- Looks good.

409
00:25:33,867 --> 00:25:35,680
What do you think of the next prototype?

410
00:25:35,680 --> 00:25:38,144
This one. Just a little more...

411
00:25:38,144 --> 00:25:39,392
- That's the one.
- And make our characters

412
00:25:39,392 --> 00:25:42,080
- more relatable.
- Well, I think it's cute.

413
00:25:42,080 --> 00:25:44,248
- It's so cute.
- Wonderful!

414
00:25:44,248 --> 00:25:45,248
It's a nice color, isn't it?

415
00:25:45,248 --> 00:25:47,372
Would you like some Zapaan?

416
00:25:54,048 --> 00:25:58,600
Zapaan...

417
00:26:04,704 --> 00:26:10,900
Well, Ms. Mondo, what do you think
of the president's methods?

418
00:26:10,900 --> 00:26:13,100
You mean Mr. Omishima?

419
00:26:13,856 --> 00:26:16,833
I agree with them, of course.

420
00:26:16,833 --> 00:26:23,567
If it weren't for him, Merry would probably
have gone bankrupt by now.

421
00:26:23,567 --> 00:26:25,467
Right.

422
00:26:26,752 --> 00:26:30,120
Thank you. This was a treat.

423
00:26:30,120 --> 00:26:36,600
Hey, Mr. Hirose, are you
still in love with Merry?

424
00:26:37,833 --> 00:26:41,800
Yes, of course. I'm head over heels.

425
00:26:41,800 --> 00:26:44,100
Be careful.

426
00:26:44,768 --> 00:26:47,340
Love is blind.

427
00:27:10,133 --> 00:27:13,167
Hey, good evening, good morning.

428
00:27:13,167 --> 00:27:16,140
Mr. Oyama was very pleased.

429
00:27:16,140 --> 00:27:18,240
Oh... Okay.

430
00:27:22,176 --> 00:27:24,972
Ah... well...

431
00:27:27,700 --> 00:27:30,528
Just to report,

432
00:27:30,528 --> 00:27:35,616
well... I didn't land the new factory line.

433
00:27:35,616 --> 00:27:38,540
Well, that's basically the gist of it.

434
00:27:41,580 --> 00:27:43,620
As originally planned,

435
00:27:43,620 --> 00:27:48,200
I think we can consider it while looking at
the condition of your hands.

436
00:27:50,567 --> 00:27:52,032
And so, there's something

437
00:27:52,032 --> 00:27:54,333
- I want to talk to you about...
- Hey.

438
00:27:55,680 --> 00:27:58,900
I'm going to go get some dinner.

439
00:27:58,900 --> 00:28:00,500
Oh, is that so?

440
00:28:00,500 --> 00:28:03,052
- Then, let's talk tomorrow or something.
- No, I mean...

441
00:28:04,224 --> 00:28:09,000
Well, you can say it to me there.

442
00:28:09,000 --> 00:28:11,100
There?

443
00:28:11,100 --> 00:28:13,420
[Asia Sunshine Market]

444
00:28:13,420 --> 00:28:17,167
Well, you said you were going to eat dinner.

445
00:28:17,900 --> 00:28:20,833
Don't you know that place?

446
00:28:25,216 --> 00:28:26,700
We're splitting the bill.

447
00:28:33,560 --> 00:28:34,976
[Kimchi]

448
00:28:34,976 --> 00:28:37,260
[Kitchen Sakoda, Mozzarella Cheese Kimchi]

449
00:28:39,500 --> 00:28:41,133
Go ahead.

450
00:28:41,133 --> 00:28:43,767
Are you sure? I'm not paying for you.

451
00:28:43,767 --> 00:28:45,867
Go ahead.

452
00:28:49,933 --> 00:28:52,167
- Open it.
- Okay.

453
00:28:57,184 --> 00:29:02,100
You really like alcohol, don't you?
It's one knowing.

454
00:29:02,100 --> 00:29:04,033
Soup?
[*"Soup" sounds like "knowing" in Japanese]

455
00:29:04,033 --> 00:29:07,400
No. "Knowing," as in "to know something."

456
00:29:08,700 --> 00:29:12,133
By the way, I'm Anna Kusakabe, 40 years old.

457
00:29:12,133 --> 00:29:14,048
From after college graduation
until very recently,

458
00:29:14,048 --> 00:29:16,200
I worked at a consulting firm.

459
00:29:16,200 --> 00:29:21,267
[Kitchen Sakoda]
And my last job was this, Kitchen Sakoda.

460
00:29:21,267 --> 00:29:23,280
It's very popular now,

461
00:29:23,280 --> 00:29:24,672
[Kitchen Sakoda]
<i>but when they came to us,</i>

462
00:29:24,672 --> 00:29:27,767
<i>it felt like a run-down Western-style diner.</i>

463
00:29:27,767 --> 00:29:32,133
<i>They came wanting to rebuild the shop,</i>

464
00:29:32,933 --> 00:29:36,067
but I thought that with this taste,
they could go even further.

465
00:29:36,067 --> 00:29:38,967
Nationally, or even globally.

466
00:29:38,967 --> 00:29:42,304
And then, I led the couple somewhere
beyond what they wanted...

467
00:29:42,304 --> 00:29:46,567
How should I say it... I misled them there?

468
00:29:46,567 --> 00:29:50,496
As a result,
the wife's business acumen awakened,

469
00:29:50,496 --> 00:29:54,080
absorbing her in the business.

470
00:29:54,080 --> 00:30:01,164
This caused a rift between them,
and they eventually ended up divorcing.

471
00:30:02,267 --> 00:30:07,060
Well, I mean, it's not like the wife
was at fault or anything.

472
00:30:07,060 --> 00:30:12,000
What happiness means to you
is something only you can decide.

473
00:30:12,000 --> 00:30:16,033
But the fact is that
I didn't respect that couple's will.

474
00:30:16,967 --> 00:30:22,284
It's a fact that I exploited
their love for their shop

475
00:30:22,944 --> 00:30:26,500
and prioritized the numbers.

476
00:30:26,500 --> 00:30:30,433
And it's also a fact that
the divorce was a result of that.

477
00:30:30,433 --> 00:30:34,800
So I quit, thinking that
I wasn't cut out for consulting.

478
00:30:35,533 --> 00:30:38,180
And yet, here I am doing it again.

479
00:30:38,180 --> 00:30:41,767
But I've decided to make this
my last consulting job.

480
00:30:41,767 --> 00:30:44,933
That, at least, I've decided, sort of.

481
00:30:44,933 --> 00:30:47,333
Wait.

482
00:30:47,333 --> 00:30:49,633
You really are a snatching hyena, aren't you?

483
00:30:49,633 --> 00:30:52,367
What? Why the personal story all of a sudden?

484
00:30:54,233 --> 00:30:58,567
The other day, watching you make revisions,

485
00:30:58,567 --> 00:31:01,024
I realized I have to know you
way more than I do now.

486
00:31:01,024 --> 00:31:03,367
About the character designer, Mizuki Seo.

487
00:31:03,367 --> 00:31:09,067
And to know you, I thought that
first, you needed to know me.

488
00:31:09,067 --> 00:31:11,008
So, since I made this,

489
00:31:11,008 --> 00:31:15,167
despite not knowing much about
the character designer Mizuki Seo,

490
00:31:15,167 --> 00:31:18,233
I'm not sure if it will be of any help.

491
00:31:19,000 --> 00:31:23,392
100 Names and Motifs Related to the Concept.

492
00:31:23,392 --> 00:31:27,533
The elements given to you
for creating the new character

493
00:31:27,533 --> 00:31:31,700
<i>are just two, "Just Support"
and "Plushie Debut," right?</i>

494
00:31:31,700 --> 00:31:35,104
So I thought this might spark
some inspiration,

495
00:31:35,104 --> 00:31:39,033
like that Zapaan moment back then.

496
00:31:39,033 --> 00:31:41,500
[Names and Motifs]

497
00:31:41,500 --> 00:31:43,300
Did you come up with this on your own?

498
00:31:43,300 --> 00:31:45,300
No, it's mainly from interviews.

499
00:31:45,300 --> 00:31:48,467
I asked various people,
"What has been the support in your life?"

500
00:31:48,467 --> 00:31:50,120
And I thought of the names.

501
00:31:50,120 --> 00:31:51,733
What about this? Number 12.

502
00:31:51,733 --> 00:31:55,320
[Mr. M, 40 years old, Office worker]
Oh, that's my boyfriend.

503
00:31:55,320 --> 00:31:57,000
<i>As for who supported him through life,</i>

504
00:31:57,000 --> 00:31:59,616
it was the one who took care of him
when he broke his nose playing kendama.

505
00:31:59,616 --> 00:32:01,700
Well... that turned out to be me.

506
00:32:01,700 --> 00:32:03,740
[Kusachingeru]
So, the name is Kusachingeru.

507
00:32:03,740 --> 00:32:06,133
Your naming sense is
kind of like an old man's.

508
00:32:06,133 --> 00:32:07,733
What?

509
00:32:07,733 --> 00:32:11,140
Well, how about this one? Number 23.

510
00:32:11,140 --> 00:32:12,257
[Mr. F, 32 years old, Office worker]
I heard it from a junior colleague back

511
00:32:12,257 --> 00:32:13,367
in my consulting days.

512
00:32:13,367 --> 00:32:17,367
The support for him was a senior colleague
who invited him to karaoke

513
00:32:17,367 --> 00:32:19,867
almost every night back
when he was a newbie making mistakes.

514
00:32:19,867 --> 00:32:21,733
Well, that was me who did that.

515
00:32:21,733 --> 00:32:23,833
[Kusakaraocation]
So, the name is Kusakaraocation.

516
00:32:23,833 --> 00:32:25,800
That sounds like an old man too.

517
00:32:25,800 --> 00:32:29,533
- How about this? Tousan-ookami...
- An old man.

518
00:32:29,533 --> 00:32:31,100
Then, this one...

519
00:32:31,100 --> 00:32:34,560
I'm sorry, but I think
we'll go with it as is.

520
00:32:34,560 --> 00:32:36,860
Is that the president's decision?

521
00:32:36,860 --> 00:32:39,596
Yes, and I agreed with it.

522
00:32:40,832 --> 00:32:43,500
Then it can't be helped, can it?

523
00:32:43,500 --> 00:32:46,733
Yes, I'm fine with that too.

524
00:32:47,600 --> 00:32:49,367
Then, let's go with this.

525
00:32:49,367 --> 00:32:51,136
Thank you for your hard work.

526
00:32:51,136 --> 00:32:53,100
Thank you for your hard work.

527
00:32:57,700 --> 00:33:03,600
It's only the first bottle...
You can't handle alcohol. It's two knowing.

528
00:33:03,600 --> 00:33:07,620
What about this? This one, this one. 93!

529
00:33:08,380 --> 00:33:11,167
[Ms. K, 54 years old]
This is a woman who sells Zapaan.

530
00:33:11,167 --> 00:33:13,933
The support for her
during hard times was Steven Seagal.

531
00:33:13,933 --> 00:33:15,533
[Silence, Motif: Steven Seagal]
So, the name is Silence.

532
00:33:15,533 --> 00:33:18,233
Enough! They're all weak!

533
00:33:18,233 --> 00:33:19,392
They don't spark anything.

534
00:33:19,392 --> 00:33:21,200
No, please try to squeeze out
some inspiration from that.

535
00:33:21,200 --> 00:33:22,400
Squeeze out some spark.

536
00:33:22,400 --> 00:33:24,467
There's no way I can
squeeze out a spark from this.

537
00:33:24,467 --> 00:33:28,333
Old man, old man, old man...

538
00:33:28,333 --> 00:33:32,233
Oh... then, how about this?
The last one, number 100!

539
00:33:32,867 --> 00:33:36,860
[Anna Kusakabe]
This is, humbly, my own.

540
00:33:36,860 --> 00:33:41,233
But I don't really have much support myself.

541
00:33:42,300 --> 00:33:45,800
It's fine. Just go ahead and tell me.

542
00:33:45,800 --> 00:33:47,667
Alright.

543
00:33:48,867 --> 00:33:54,784
I'm the eldest of four siblings,
and we have a single parent,

544
00:33:54,784 --> 00:33:56,633
so looking after others just became natural.

545
00:33:56,633 --> 00:34:01,033
So I didn't have the time or money
to get really into anything.

546
00:34:01,033 --> 00:34:04,900
And I think it was when I was in 5th grade.

547
00:34:05,800 --> 00:34:07,567
For a composition homework,

548
00:34:07,567 --> 00:34:11,667
the teacher said, "The topic
is your dream for the future."

549
00:34:11,667 --> 00:34:13,800
I was like, "Oh, no."

550
00:34:13,800 --> 00:34:16,800
Because I had never thought
about a dream before,

551
00:34:16,800 --> 00:34:22,112
I decided to borrow a bit from
my cousin's dream of becoming a ballerina.

552
00:34:22,112 --> 00:34:25,933
Oh, so you've had talent since way back then.

553
00:34:25,933 --> 00:34:28,876
A talent for being sneaky.

554
00:34:29,867 --> 00:34:34,133
So I wrote it up enthusiastically,
like a presentation.

555
00:34:34,767 --> 00:34:37,233
"My dream for the future
is to be a ballerina."

556
00:34:37,233 --> 00:34:40,800
"I haven't done it yet,
but I'd like to try someday."

557
00:34:40,800 --> 00:34:42,176
With tear stains and such.

558
00:34:42,176 --> 00:34:45,967
I know that. Elementary school...
those tear stains.

559
00:34:45,967 --> 00:34:47,600
And the snot that comes with it.

560
00:34:47,600 --> 00:34:49,733
When you occasionally lick it,
the saltiness...

561
00:34:49,733 --> 00:34:54,733
And that essay ended up
winning the Vice Principal's Award.

562
00:34:54,733 --> 00:34:57,100
Unexpectedly, my mom got to see it.

563
00:34:57,100 --> 00:34:59,667
[Spicy Chips]
My mom cried her eyes out.

564
00:34:59,667 --> 00:35:03,000
Do you know what a ballet tutu is?
That frilly skirt-like thing.

565
00:35:03,000 --> 00:35:04,967
My mom bought that for me.

566
00:35:04,967 --> 00:35:08,520
She said, "I'm sorry.
I made you give up on your dream."

567
00:35:08,520 --> 00:35:13,467
Seeing that, I also started
crying my eyes out.

568
00:35:13,467 --> 00:35:18,600
"Sorry, it's a lie. I don't actually
have a dream or anything."

569
00:35:18,600 --> 00:35:22,867
And that fact then spread
throughout the whole school.

570
00:35:22,867 --> 00:35:24,160
So that's what it was like.

571
00:35:24,160 --> 00:35:28,020
[Jigoku-guruma Ballerina]
So the name is Jigoku-guruma Ballerina.

572
00:35:29,033 --> 00:35:32,033
I think, somewhere along the way,

573
00:35:32,033 --> 00:35:36,623
I'd grown bitter about having no dreams,
nothing I truly loved.

574
00:35:37,312 --> 00:35:40,833
Well, but the other day,
Mr. Hirose said something.

575
00:35:40,833 --> 00:35:44,396
That love or passion is like lightning...

576
00:35:47,133 --> 00:35:49,400
What? I can't see anything, I can't see.

577
00:35:49,400 --> 00:35:52,700
Wait a moment... Well...

578
00:35:53,900 --> 00:35:56,000
Oh, my!

579
00:35:56,800 --> 00:35:58,933
What, what, what?

580
00:35:58,933 --> 00:36:02,300
Wait! J-J-Just a moment...

581
00:36:14,528 --> 00:36:16,567
Mr. Seo!

582
00:36:17,888 --> 00:36:20,012
Where are you going?

583
00:36:21,267 --> 00:36:24,268
- Ouch! That hurt...
- Mr. Hirose?

584
00:36:25,952 --> 00:36:28,067
- Are you okay? Here.
- Ouch, ouch, ouch...

585
00:36:28,067 --> 00:36:29,408
Please, have a seat on this chair!

586
00:36:29,408 --> 00:36:30,967
- Here. Here.
- Ouch, ouch... Thank you.

587
00:36:30,967 --> 00:36:32,933
Before that... the bread. The bread...

588
00:36:32,933 --> 00:36:35,700
Oh, the bread! I'm sorry.

589
00:36:35,700 --> 00:36:37,388
Will you be alright?

590
00:36:40,128 --> 00:36:44,900
Could it be that lightning struck his brain?

591
00:36:44,900 --> 00:36:46,533
It's fine.

592
00:36:46,533 --> 00:36:48,267
Something like this happened once before.

593
00:36:48,267 --> 00:36:52,200
That's when Full Tummy Molco was born.

594
00:36:56,367 --> 00:37:03,667
When Mr. Seo left Merry,
I sided with the president, not him.

595
00:37:05,536 --> 00:37:07,867
Do you know why?

596
00:37:09,568 --> 00:37:12,524
I was scared of getting fired.

597
00:37:13,533 --> 00:37:18,988
At that time at Merry, the president
was steadily laying off long-time employees.

598
00:37:20,000 --> 00:37:24,267
I was terribly afraid of being thrown out
into the world at this age.

599
00:37:25,133 --> 00:37:28,033
But he was strong.

600
00:37:28,933 --> 00:37:32,556
His love makes him strong.

601
00:37:33,533 --> 00:37:35,372
I envy that.

602
00:37:37,600 --> 00:37:40,067
Only you, Mr. Seo.

603
00:37:41,536 --> 00:37:43,276
It's unfair, isn't it?

604
00:37:44,500 --> 00:37:48,733
But you told me before,
didn't you, Mr. Hirose?

605
00:37:48,733 --> 00:37:51,533
That love is like lightning.

606
00:37:52,320 --> 00:37:57,696
I felt saved by those words.

607
00:37:57,696 --> 00:38:01,567
Because lightning is just
a matter of luck, isn't it?

608
00:38:01,567 --> 00:38:07,267
And if so, until that day comes,
I just have to do what I can.

609
00:38:07,267 --> 00:38:13,100
So, in this situation,
I can't imagine what I can do right now,

610
00:38:13,100 --> 00:38:14,720
but I'll be going now.

611
00:38:14,720 --> 00:38:16,020
Take your time.

612
00:38:17,833 --> 00:38:22,333
- Well, shall I wipe your sweat?
- No need.

613
00:38:22,333 --> 00:38:24,888
- Get my phone.
- Your phone... Here you go.

614
00:38:24,888 --> 00:38:25,888
Photo.

615
00:38:25,888 --> 00:38:28,064
- <i>Here, I feel like the squishiness</i>
- Photo.

616
00:38:28,064 --> 00:38:30,208
<i>of these ears will weaken
once it's made into a plushie.</i>

617
00:38:30,208 --> 00:38:32,256
<i>Then I can just make the contrast stronger.</i>

618
00:38:32,256 --> 00:38:33,740
<i>That's right.</i>

619
00:38:36,096 --> 00:38:39,133
- This one?
- Yes. Zoom in on the ears.

620
00:38:43,633 --> 00:38:45,933
Not yet.

621
00:38:45,933 --> 00:38:47,820
I haven't done anything yet.

622
00:38:49,664 --> 00:38:51,968
- Be closer to eyes.
- Be close to eyes?

623
00:38:51,968 --> 00:38:54,380
- My eyes. Can't see.
- My eyes?

624
00:39:06,016 --> 00:39:08,067
I hope your work went well.

625
00:39:09,800 --> 00:39:11,633
Mr. Omishima!

626
00:39:15,733 --> 00:39:17,700
I have something to discuss.

627
00:39:23,367 --> 00:39:25,233
Here you go.

628
00:39:28,000 --> 00:39:29,867
It's at 11 o'clock, right?

629
00:39:30,833 --> 00:39:33,767
I'll definitely make it in time.

630
00:39:33,767 --> 00:39:35,700
The plushie factory.

631
00:39:36,667 --> 00:39:40,567
Could we please have them made here?

632
00:39:41,600 --> 00:39:43,267
I'm pretty sure I already told you this.

633
00:39:43,267 --> 00:39:46,485
- Nobody will notice a 1mm difference.
- The products we create

634
00:39:46,485 --> 00:39:53,785
can stay with a customer
for days, years, even decades.

635
00:39:55,136 --> 00:39:58,233
They can even become a lifelong love.

636
00:39:59,033 --> 00:40:02,100
We are engaged in work that holds
such extraordinary dreams

637
00:40:02,100 --> 00:40:04,567
and is incredibly wonderful.

638
00:40:04,567 --> 00:40:09,300
That's exactly why it comes with
immense responsibility.

639
00:40:10,100 --> 00:40:13,933
Merry is a company that delivers love.

640
00:40:13,933 --> 00:40:16,180
We won't allow even a 1mm compromise.

641
00:40:16,180 --> 00:40:22,220
I believe that this very attitude
is connected to Merry's future.

642
00:40:32,733 --> 00:40:34,743
[Save]

643
00:40:38,033 --> 00:40:41,600
Why don't we just forget about
the deadline for a moment?

644
00:40:41,600 --> 00:40:44,000
No, no. If we miss this, it could be years.

645
00:40:44,000 --> 00:40:46,830
But the character you're drawing now,

646
00:40:47,440 --> 00:40:51,107
is it really turning out
to be like your Berry?

647
00:40:53,567 --> 00:40:56,700
A character that will always support someone.

648
00:40:57,900 --> 00:40:59,788
Is it becoming that kind of character?

649
00:41:03,700 --> 00:41:06,067
If not,

650
00:41:06,067 --> 00:41:09,033
if you're just putting it out
because of the deadline,

651
00:41:09,033 --> 00:41:12,200
isn't that already not being a pro?

652
00:41:12,200 --> 00:41:14,560
Don't worry about the factory.

653
00:41:14,560 --> 00:41:17,824
After all, how many such manufacturers
do you think there are nationwide?

654
00:41:17,824 --> 00:41:20,367
We just need to contact all of them.

655
00:41:20,367 --> 00:41:23,596
That's what a pro does.

656
00:41:26,467 --> 00:41:28,233
I see.

657
00:41:29,700 --> 00:41:33,996
It seems there is a significant
difference of opinion between us.

658
00:41:34,800 --> 00:41:36,800
Difference?

659
00:41:36,800 --> 00:41:41,056
The character business industry today
places a heavy emphasis on going viral.

660
00:41:41,056 --> 00:41:44,600
Trends and fads come and go
at a terrifying speed.

661
00:41:44,600 --> 00:41:48,333
This is even more so
for a non-mainstream character like Molco.

662
00:41:48,333 --> 00:41:51,167
Probably in about two years,
the entire country will be tired of it,

663
00:41:51,167 --> 00:41:53,433
and sales will plummet.

664
00:41:53,433 --> 00:41:56,480
That's why we're doping it early
with an even stronger quirk,

665
00:41:56,480 --> 00:41:58,592
Harsh Review Molco.

666
00:41:58,592 --> 00:42:01,760
But even if you do that,
it will only create a temporary buzz.

667
00:42:01,760 --> 00:42:04,300
Because it will disappear someday anyway.

668
00:42:08,267 --> 00:42:09,800
Understood.

669
00:42:09,800 --> 00:42:11,600
Alright, then.

670
00:42:16,967 --> 00:42:20,108
I'm sorry, Ms. Sasaoka.
Can I ask you to do a few things?

671
00:42:20,800 --> 00:42:22,400
I am very sorry.

672
00:42:22,400 --> 00:42:27,000
Now, now, it was a long shot
from the beginning.

673
00:42:29,952 --> 00:42:31,980
[Hasegawa Textile Studio Co., Ltd.]

674
00:42:37,800 --> 00:42:39,776
[Resignation Letter]

675
00:42:39,776 --> 00:42:41,667
I see.

676
00:42:41,667 --> 00:42:46,200
Can't believe you're job hunting at this age.

677
00:42:46,200 --> 00:42:51,700
But the world isn't that easy, ribbit.

678
00:42:52,800 --> 00:42:58,000
At the very least, no company
pays as well as we do, ribbit.

679
00:42:58,000 --> 00:43:01,804
Especially for someone
nearing retirement, ribbit.

680
00:43:05,933 --> 00:43:08,620
I've reserved our usual place for 8 o'clock.

681
00:43:11,700 --> 00:43:14,367
I've secured a private room in the back.

682
00:43:17,568 --> 00:43:20,133
Alright, I'll go pull the car
up to the front entrance.

683
00:43:24,704 --> 00:43:26,476
I'm back.

684
00:44:04,433 --> 00:44:06,500
It's done.

685
00:44:07,936 --> 00:44:10,233
It's done. It's done!

686
00:44:39,267 --> 00:44:41,767
It struck! Lightning struck!

687
00:44:46,560 --> 00:44:47,920
I love...
